{
  "beruf": "bau-handwerk",
  "title": "Construction and trades",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "italian-for-work run, 2026-11-15: written by hand for Italy, not yet read by a human or a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation. Working conditions are general orientation only; facts marked 'to verify' in the run report.",
  "intro": "Building sites and trades (muratore, idraulico, elettricista, imbianchino) are a common way into work in Italy. This deck has the cantiere and its tools, the jobs you do there, the short orders you will hear, safety equipment, and the words around your contract and pay. On site colleagues say tu, with the client you say Lei, and instructions are short.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work on a building site or do repairs in Italy? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Manovale",
    "Muratore",
    "Imbianchino",
    "Idraulico",
    "Elettricista",
    "Piastrellista",
    "Carpentiere"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "Orders on site are short: «Sali!», «Scendi!», «Fermo!», «Più a destra!». If you don't understand, ask before you move: «Scusa, non ho capito. Puoi ripetere?»",
    "Don't work without your DPI (helmet, safety boots, gloves, harness at height). Asking «Dov'è il casco?» is normal, not a complaint.",
    "Construction work in Italy requires safety training before you start on site (formazione sulla sicurezza). Ask the employer which courses and certificates you need and who pays for them. This is general orientation, not legal advice.",
    "Work without a contract or a payslip (lavoro in nero) is illegal and leaves you without insurance if you are hurt. If you are unsure, ask a union (sindacato/patronato) or the Ispettorato del lavoro. The rules on pay, hours and holidays depend on your contract (CCNL); this is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
  "links": [
